<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Normandy landings</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1944</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as D-Day, Operation Neptune · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Normandy landings of 6 June 1944, known as D-Day, were the Allied amphibious and airborne invasion of German-occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> that opened Operation Overlord. American, British and Canadian forces landed on five beaches, code-named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a>, Omaha, Gold, Juno and Sword, after airborne drops behind them. It was the largest seaborne invasion in history and led to the liberation of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/normandy-landings.jpg" alt="Normandy landings" width="320" height="258" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chief Photographer's Mate (CPHoM) Robert F. Sargent · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Into_the_Jaws_of_Death_23-0455M_edit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
